ghudson@CIE.UOREGON.EDU writes:

> ... it seems like a pretty dumb way to lose
>a character that your pet, normally a reliable check, suddenly decides
>it's going to ignore its instincts.

I disagree.  As the fortune cookie says, dogs are superstitious.  Now, a
superstition is going to make you want to stay away, but no animal is a
geiger counter.  I haven't examined the code (probably just as well), but
I find I have to sort of get a 'feeling' from the pet's behavior.  I much
prefer this.

Which reminds me of something else:  Altars should not be geiger counters
either.  If you were a God, wouldn't you resent your sacred place being
used in such a mechanical manner?  I think, depending on how tight you are
with the Gods, items should perhaps be blessed or cursed upon striking the
altar (small probability), or you might anger the Gods.

			if the Gods dig you:

The sword glows black as it hits the altar. --more--
Suddenly, the sword gives off a radiant white light. --more--
You feel full of awe.

			or, if you are not so lucky:

The crystal ball glows black as it lands on the altar. --more--
You hear a clap of thunder.  A voice booms out --more--
"How dare you place a cursed item on my sacred altar.  You will pay for this!"

I am not out to make the game harder, so much as more logical.  To me the
most incredibly exciting thing about nethack is that you really can use
your head, guess what the outcome of something would logically be, and
figure out how to proceed.  The game has its own bizarre logic.

